# Chapter 1: The Letter (ticket 0707).
# Beat sheet: docs/story/chapters/ch01.md. Continuity: docs/story/ledger.md.
#
# Scene ids, for 0803 to wire into chapters/ch01.ron and battles/ch01.ron:
#
#   Before the battle (intro_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_intro                 the opening caption, then the letter
#     ch01_prebattle             the Red Company; the party gathers
#
#   In the battle (triggers, each once)
#     ch01_first_turn            TurnStart(turn: 1, phase: Player)
#     ch01_boss_engage_lead      CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("lead"))
#     ch01_boss_engage_sergeant  CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("sergeant"))
#     ch01_boss_engage           CombatStart(unit: "red_captain"): anyone else
#     ch01_boss_half             HalfHp(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_red_captain     UnitFell(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_lead            UnitFell(unit: "lead"): game over in both modes
#     ch01_death_<id>            UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Classic))
#     ch01_retreat_<id>          UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Casual))
#       for <id> = retainer, sergeant, poacher, keeper, heretic
#
#   After the battle (victory_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_victory
#     ch01_tbc
#
# The lead has three reply choices: one each in ch01_intro, ch01_prebattle
# and ch01_victory. Aske counts "twelve men, no, fourteen": the map's enemy
# count should not contradict it (chapter-1.md allows 12-14 and the boss).
#
# ch01_victory is written for a battle everyone lived through. A companion
# who died in Classic still speaks in it until 0715 and 0716.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_intro
> Three years after {n:event.ash_peace}.
> The war is won. The soldiers who won it were never paid.
> In {n:place.thornmarch}, a long way from any court, an exile chops wood.
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, {n:place.thornmarch}
@left  lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
> Dawn, in the yard behind a rented house. Two practice swords crack
  together, wood on wood, as they have every morning for four years.
retainer: Guard up, {n:family.veyne}. Higher. That eyebrow's had one scar
  off me already, and I'm not proud of it.
retainer[happy]: A bit proud.
lead: Again.
> Three strokes. The fourth takes the old man's sword out of his hand.
retainer[surprised]: ...Huh. About time. Pick that up for me. My knees
  won't.
retainer[sad]: Then sit down, kid. A pedlar left this last night. No seal,
  no name. I know the hand. So will you.
> "Leave the march. Tonight. They are coming for everyone who was there
  that night. Burn this."
lead: {n:rival.first}.
retainer: Four years, and not a word. Not to you. Not to his own father.
  Then that.
@choice
* earnest: He's warning us. He still cares, {n:retainer.first}.
  retainer[sad]: Aye. That's what worries me.
* wry: Four years of silence, and now he's urgent.
  retainer[happy]: Ha. He always did write like a man owed money.
  retainer[sad]: He's not the one who's owed.
* blunt: What does he want?
  retainer[angry]: Us gone, by the look of it. What I'd ask is who's
    coming, and how he knows.
@endchoice
retainer[sad]: "Everyone who was there." That's you. That's me. That's
  him. And my girl, and she's past anyone coming for her.
> He does not burn the letter. He folds it small, and puts it back inside
  his coat.
> Hooves on the ford road, coming hard.
retainer[neutral]: That's the sergeant. She doesn't ride like that for good
  news.
@end

@scene ch01_prebattle
@caption The green, {n:place.harrowby}
@left  retainer neutral
@right sergeant angry
sergeant: Captain! Your Former Grace! {N:red_captain.nickname} and his whole
  sorry company took the toll-fort in the night. I rode up to ask why, and
  he offered me my old job back.
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Sergeant! We heard the horse from the chapel. Is someone hurt? I've
  brought bandages, and {n:heretic}, to, ah, to carry the bandages.
sergeant[neutral]: Nobody's hurt yet, Keeper. He has a paper, with a lord's
  seal he kept his thumb on, and three people he's paid to find. "The exile,
  the old man, and the girl with the burned hands."
sergeant[happy]: I'm not on it. I checked twice. That's going in the ledger,
  under insults.
keeper: Burned... but I only asked them to... Oh. Oh, {n:god.mother.name}.
@right heretic angry
heretic: What? What's everyone looking at? They're hands. And don't you
  stare, heir. It's you at the top of that paper, not me.
> {N:retainer.first} has not said a word. He turns, and walks back toward
  the house.
> Something drops out of the chapel yew: a thin boy, a long bow, two
  rabbits.
@left  poacher neutral
@right sergeant happy
poacher: Twelve men. No. Fourteen. Bad archers.
sergeant: Morning, {n:faction.brennmark.adj}. Up a tree all night, counting
  brigands for us? I'm touched.
poacher[angry]: Nobody has touched you. And it is not for you. They killed
  three deer last night and left two to rot. In my wood. I will help you,
  noble. Once. For the deer.
@left  keeper sad
@right heretic angry
keeper: {n:heretic}, the chapel cellar bars from the inside. I would ask
  you to go down. I do know, with respect, what you are about to say.
heretic: I'm not a cook, Keeper. I'm a girl who can light the oven without a
  match. Different thing.
@left  lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
> {N:retainer.first} comes back across the green with a long bundle of
  oilcloth. It has been under the floorboards since the day they came.
retainer: Wooden swords. Four years of wooden swords. Try not to cut
  yourself on the real one.
> {lead} takes the sword. The terms of {their} exile forbid it.
retainer[sad]: They've come for three of us. There are forty people between
  here and that fort who never did a thing to anyone. Your call,
  {n:family.veyne}.
@choice
* earnest: We keep them away from the village.
  @right poacher neutral
  poacher: The miller's wife buys my rabbits. She pays too much. She thinks
    I do not notice. I notice. Keep them away from her.
  @right sergeant neutral
* wry: Only fourteen? I'm insulted.
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: Ha! Finally, somebody said it. I've seen that lot fight, Your
    Former Grace. Half of them couldn't hit the river from the bridge.
* blunt: We hit the fort first.
  @right heretic angry
  heretic: Giving orders already, heir? Pity. It's the one I'd have given.
  @right sergeant neutral
@endchoice
sergeant[happy]: Fourteen of {n:red_captain.first}'s finest, against an
  exile, an old man, a keeper, a cook, a poacher and me. Put it on the
  crown's tab.
lead: Let's move.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_first_turn
@left retainer neutral
retainer: Stay close, kid. The first real one's always ugly.
@end

# Harl's first fight. A scene written for him and one character replaces
# the general one (docs/design/battle-scenes-and-recruitment.md). The
# general one meets Hollis and Rue, who are on his paper, and Aske and
# Piers, who aren't.

@scene ch01_boss_engage
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: Three heads on my paper, three hundred crowns a head. If yours
  isn't one of them, you're dying for nothing.
red_captain[angry]: The war taught me not to mind which. Come on, then.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_lead
@left  lead neutral
@right red_captain happy
red_captain: Nothing personal, Your Grace. You're three hundred crowns and a
  hot meal for my lads.
lead: Who's paying?
red_captain[neutral]: Ask me when I'm dead.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_sergeant
@left  sergeant neutral
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: {n:sergeant.last}. You left before pay-day. Bad habit.
sergeant[angry]: I left after the farm, {n:red_captain.first}. You know I
  did.
red_captain: A lord paid for that farm. A lord's paying for this. Somebody
  has to pay for something.
sergeant[sad]: Then it's your bill, {n:red_captain.first}. I stopped signing
  for it last winter.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_half
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: Hah. I bled worse than this at the passes, for a king who never
  learned my name.
red_captain: I'll not die owed. Not here. Not by you.
@end

# Said across the field: the lead may be nowhere near him.

@scene ch01_death_red_captain
@right red_captain sad
red_captain: Hah... Still owed. Figures.
red_captain: Your Grace! One thing, free of charge. The new lord of
  {n:family.veyne} pays in good silver.
red_captain: Your own crest on the order, and everything. Must be nice...
  having friends.
@end

# The lead's fall is game over in both modes.

@scene ch01_death_lead
@left lead sad
> The letter said to run. {lead} stayed.
@end

# The companions: a death quote in Classic, a retreat line in Casual
# (docs/design/death-and-difficulty.md). They can fire in any order, so
# none of them speaks to another companion by name.

@scene ch01_death_retainer
@left retainer sad
retainer: Hnh. Guard was low. Mine, this time.
retainer: Eyes front, {lead}. And if you ever see the boy again, tell
  him... No. Leave it. He knows.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_retainer
@left retainer angry
retainer: That's me done. Fifty-six, and beaten off a field by my own knees.
retainer[neutral]: Guard up, {n:family.veyne}. Finish it. I'll have the
  kettle on.
@end

@scene ch01_death_sergeant
@left sergeant sad
sergeant: Ah. That'll cost us.
sergeant: Take the ledger off my belt, Your Former Grace. Four thousand, two
  hundred and twelve crowns. Don't let them off a single one.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
sergeant: That's a hole in me and a hole in the jacket. I only own the one
  jacket.
sergeant[neutral]: I'm out, Your Former Grace. Try not to win it too cheaply
  without me.
@end

@scene ch01_death_poacher
@left poacher surprised
poacher: I counted wrong. There was one more.
poacher[sad]: My silver is in my left boot. Send it north, noble. To my
  {n:word.mother}. You owe me that.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: I am hit. It is not deep. I will go and sit behind the mill and be
  angry.
poacher: Do not lose, noble. I will know.
@end

@scene ch01_death_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Oh. Oh, dear. That is... that is supposed to be on the inside.
keeper[sad]: {N:god.mother.name} keep you all. I did a thing this autumn,
  and I am sorry for it. I had hoped to say so myself, to the one it hurt.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: I'm hurt. I think. Yes. No, don't look, I'm the keeper, I'll look.
keeper[sad]: I must withdraw. {N:god.mother.name} keep you. Also, please,
  keep pressure on anything that leaks.
@end

@scene ch01_death_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: {N:god.pyre.name}'s teeth. Typical. First time in years I ate
  every day.
heretic[sad]: Don't stare, heir. The Keeper's stew was all right. It wasn't.
  I'd have told him it was.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: Ow! {N:god.pyre.name}'s teeth! Fine. Fine! I'm going.
heretic: Don't you dare lose while I'm not looking, heir.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_victory
@caption The toll-fort, noon
> Behind them, one barn smokes at the edge of {n:place.harrowby}. Only the
  one.
@left  lead neutral
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: Pay-day at last, {n:red_captain.first}. I'll mark the ledger: paid
  in full.
> Nobody laughs. She doesn't either. She crouches, closes his eyes with two
  fingers, and takes a folded paper from inside his jacket.
sergeant[sad]: ...Just a job. That's all it was.
> His orders: three hundred crowns a head for the exile, the old man and
  the girl. The seal is blue wax. The crest in it is the one {lead} cut from
  {their} own coat four years ago.
sergeant[neutral]: He wasn't lying, then. That's your crest, Your Former
  Grace. I thought they took it off you with the house.
@left keeper sad
keeper: They did. The seal goes with the title. Nobody may use it but the
  lord of {n:family.veyne}, and the lord of {n:family.veyne}, these three
  years... Captain, I am sorry.
> Everyone looks at {n:retainer.first}. {N:retainer.first} looks at the wax,
  and says nothing at all.
sergeant: {N:rival}, hero of the war. The Captain's boy. Somebody say
  something. I'm no good in a quiet.
@right heretic neutral
heretic: I'll say something. I've seen that seal before, heir. On a cart. At
  night. With children in it.
@left lead neutral
lead: Where?
heretic[angry]: You tell me. Four years ago it was your family's to stamp.
@left keeper sad
keeper: Please. Not here. Somebody was told that {n:heretic} is in
  {n:place.harrowby}, and only {n:faction.vigil} hunts a girl for her hands.
  They will send others.
@left poacher neutral
poacher: Then I come also. You owe me eleven arrows, noble.
heretic[neutral]: I'm not following you, heir. I'm walking behind you. Where
  I can see your hands.
@right sergeant happy
sergeant: And I'll ride along as far as there's pay. ...Further, probably.
@left  lead neutral
@right retainer sad
retainer: The boy's at {n:place.veyne_hall}, kid. One step off the march to
  ask him and you're an outlaw, for good. Say the word anyway.
@choice
* earnest: He'll tell me why. I have to believe that.
  retainer: Aye. I'd like to believe it too.
* wry: I've always wanted to be an outlaw.
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: It's soldiering with worse food and better company. You'll take
    to it.
  @right retainer sad
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. We leave now.
  @right poacher neutral
  poacher: Good. This fort smells of them.
  @right retainer sad
@endchoice
retainer[neutral]: Go and pack. I'll find a spade. I've buried better men
  than {n:red_captain}. I'll dig this one's hole myself. It's the least I
  can do for him.
> By dusk they are on the road out of {n:place.harrowby}. Past the boundary
  stone, every step {lead} takes is against the king's law.
@end

@scene ch01_tbc
@caption {N:place.veyne_hall}
> Night. The old lord's study. The banner of {n:house.veyne} hangs where it
  always hung, over a man who was not born under it.
@left  rival neutral
@right messenger neutral
messenger: {N:vowmaster.title} asks whether it's done.
> {N:rival.first} reads the report on his desk again. Three lines.
  {N:faction.red_company} is dead, to a man. The exile is not.
rival: ...Tell him it's done.
@right clear
> The messenger goes. {N:rival.first} burns the report in the candle flame,
  then looks down for a long time at the blue sash across his chest, and
  the full crest of {n:house.veyne} on it.
rival[sad]: I told you to run, {n:family.veyne}.
@end
